  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nMoving into the second half of the week, the broad mid level trough \nwill remain nearby on Thursday, however, mid level ridging in the \nwestern Atlantic will start to build closer to South Florida heading \ninto Friday. At the surface, the stalled out frontal boundary will \nlinger close to the region over Central Florida before slowly trying \nto lift back northward heading into Friday. This would be in \nresponse to a broad area of low pressure trying to develop in the \nSouthwestern Gulf and the Bay of Campeche. The National Hurricane \nCenter gives this broad area of low pressure a high chance (80%) \nof development over the next 48 hours as it slowly drifts east \nnortheastward during this time frame. With the front remaining \nclose by on Thursday, deep tropical moisture will still continue \nto advect into the region, keeping enhanced rainfall chances in \nplace over most of the area. The potential for localized flooding\nwill also remain in place especially along and north of Alligator\nalley which will be closer to the front during this time frame. \n\nThe forecast heading into Friday and the first part of the weekend \nstill remains uncertain as the broad area of low pressure \ncurrently in the southwestern Gulf will ultimately play a role in \nlifting the stalled frontal boundary further north of the region. \nThe latest global and ensemble guidance suite remain in \ndisagreement in regards to timing and intensity of this area of \nlow pressure as it gradually pulls northward towards the central \nGulf Coast states for the end of the week and into the upcoming \nweekend. If the low is slower to move north northeastward, the \nfront could remain closer to the region drawing in additional \ndeep layer moisture for showers and thunderstorms to develop on \nFriday and Saturday. In addition to this, the developing system \ncould draw up some more moisture from the Carribean into South \nFlorida during this time frame. With all of these variables in \nplace for Friday and Saturday, the latest forecast keeps higher \nend chances of showers and thunderstorms over the region during \nthis time frame. High temperatures for Friday and Saturday will \nrise into the upper 80s to around 90 across most of the region. \n\nGuidance does show some signals of a pattern change heading towards \nthe second half of the weekend and into early next week as mid level \nridging strengthens over the western Atlantic and tries to build \nover the Florida Peninsula. This could introduce some drier air\ninto the region during this time frame bringing the chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms back to a typical late wet season type \npattern with increased southeasterly wind flow. The latest \nforecast reflects this and keeps the higher chances of showers \nand thunderstorms confined to interior locations as well as \nSouthwestern Florida. High temperatures look to be near or \nslightly above climatological normals during this time frame with \nridging building back into the region.",
